Title = {Arraigo with the river: Cracks of care amidst water violences along the
   Arenal River, Colombia},
Journal = {ENVIRONMENT AND PLANNING E-NATURE AND SPACE},
Year = {2026},
Month = {2026 AUG 6},
Abstract = {This article examines how rural youth in the Arenal River, Colombia are
   forging modes of arraigo {[}rootedness] through care practices amidst
   overlapping violences. Through action-research with young people engaged
   in community-tourism, we explore how care for the river enacts an
   ethico-political practice that confronts what we term `water violences':
   the accumulated effects of the modern/colonial order materialized in
   hydroextractivism, armed conflict, and mass tourism. Engaging with an
   interdisciplinary framework that draws on Latin American decolonial and
   feminist thought, alongside care scholarship and political ecology, we
   introduce the notion of `cracks of care' to address how everyday
   practices and relationality open spaces of resistance and river-making.
   These cracks reveal emergent subjective formations among rural youth who
   articulate their connection to the river through living arraigo from a
   situated understanding of care. By learning to live with -rather than
   simply in- the river, youth are actively resignifying and recomposing
   their lifeways and their river-worlds, negotiating the present and
   afterlives of water violences while crafting alternative territorial
   imaginaries. Our analysis shows how cracks of care unfold as an
   entanglement of practices, emotions, memories, and stories, that expand
   existing understandings of river-worlds, care, violence, and rural youth
   subjectivities in contexts of multifarious crisis.},
