Journal = {ACTA PSYCHOLOGICA},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {261},
Month = {NOV},
Abstract = {As urbanization and climate challenges intensify, the need for
   sustainable urban transportation becomes increasingly urgent. Electric
   ride-hailing presents a cleaner mobility solution, yet its adoption in
   developing countries like Vietnam remains limited. This study
   investigated the intention of Generation Z (Gen Z) consumers in Vietnam
   to adopt electric ride-hailing services (ERHS), integrating
   environmental, social, and governance (ESG) perceptions with key
   individual psychological traits. Grounded in the
   Stimulus-Organism-Response (SOR) framework, the research employed a
   quantitative approach, analyzed data from 411 Gen Z respondents using
   partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM). The
   proposed model examined how ESG dimensions (environmental, social, and
   governance responsibility) and personal factors (environmental
   selfidentity, personal innovativeness, and personal norms) influence
   consumer attitudes, perceived trust, and perceived value, which in turn
   affect their intention to use ERHS. The findings revealed that both ESG
   perceptions and personal characteristics significantly shape consumer
   evaluations and behavioral intentions. Environmental self-identity
   emerged as the most influential predictor across consumer assessments,
   while perceived value was the strongest determinant of usage intention.
   Interestingly, personal innovativeness had a significant effect on
   perceived trust but did not impact attitude or perceived value,
   suggesting that openness to innovation alone may not drive adoption in
   early-stage markets. These insights offer practical implications for
   service providers and policymakers, emphasizing the importance of
   authentic environmental commitments, transparent governance, and
   value-aligned marketing strategies. The study contributes to the
   theoretical discourse on ESGdriven consumer behavior and provides
   actionable guidance for promoting sustainable urban mobility in emerging
   economies.},
